---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import ControlStrip from '../components/ControlStrip.astro';
import DownloadCards from '../components/DownloadCards.astro';
import Feature from '../components/Feature.astro';
import InstallCommand from '../components/InstallCommand.astro';
import SectionHead from '../components/SectionHead.astro';
import { base } from '../lib/base.js';
import { products } from '../lib/marks.js';
import { download, downloads as loadDownloads, github, marketplace, pages, setupYaml, workbenchNav } from '../lib/workbench.js';
// Written by screenshots/capture.cjs: the overview's numbered regions and
// the toolbar strip's crops and hotspots, as percentages of each image.
import screenshots from '../data/screenshots.json';

const downloads = await loadDownloads();
const version = downloads && downloads.version;
const mark = products.find(p => p.name === 'Workbench');

const statesYaml = `- label: Sign in
  src: pages/sign-in.html
  states:
    - id: default
      label: Default
    - id: error
      label: Wrong password`;
---
<Base
  title="Canonic Workbench"
  description="A VS Code extension that renders a project's pages and component previews on one canvas at device widths, with markup and screenshots for coding agents."
  product="Workbench"
  version={version}
  nav={workbenchNav('Overview')}
  github={github}
  download={download}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="hero-text">
        <a class="chip" href="#tour">
          <svg viewBox={mark.viewBox} aria-hidden="true"><path d={mark.base} fill="currentColor" /><path d={mark.accent} class="accent" /></svg>
          Canonic Workbench{version && ` v${version}`}
        </a>
        <h1>Preview your project’s pages and components in VS Code.</h1>
        <p>A VS Code extension that renders HTML pages, TypeScript component previews, Storybook stories, and running apps on one canvas at device widths. You can draw on a screen and copy the marks, with a screenshot, as a prompt for a coding agent.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn" href="#install">Install</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#tour">Interface</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={pages.docs}>Docs</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="window">
        <div class="window-bar" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span>
          <span class="addr">#pages/sign-in.html</span>
        </div>
        <div class="shot pinned">
          <img src={`${base}images/overview.jpg`} width="1440" height="860" alt="Workbench: a screen list on the left with an Auth folder and a Sign in page, and the Acme sign-in page on the canvas at full width.">
          {screenshots.regions.map(region => (
            <>
              <span class="region" style={`left: ${region.box.left}%; top: ${region.box.top}%; width: ${region.box.width}%; height: ${region.box.height}%`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span class="pin" style={`left: ${region.pin.left}%; top: ${region.pin.top}%`} title={region.name} aria-hidden="true">{region.n}</span>
            </>
          ))}
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="tour" class="tour band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="toolbar-block">
        <SectionHead kicker="Interface" title="Layout">
          Workbench reads <code>workbench.yaml</code> at the root of your project. The sidebar lists the pages it declares, the TypeScript previews it finds, and the screens from any implementation catalogs you enable, such as a Storybook. Matching section and folder names bring those screens together in one list.
        </SectionHead>
        <ol class="areas">
          <li><span class="n">01</span><h3>Screen list</h3><p>The sections in <code>workbench.yaml</code>, such as Pages and Components, at the top; the selected section’s screens and folders below; search at the bottom. A screen’s states are listed under it. In VS Code the list is in the sidebar, under a switcher for the projects you’ve added.</p></li>
          <li><span class="n">02</span><h3>Top bar</h3><p>The screen and its state on the left, then the Actions toggle. The lens switcher in the middle; frame sizes and screen actions on the right. Controls that don’t fit move into the More menu.</p></li>
          <li><span class="n">03</span><h3>Canvas</h3><p>The screen, rendered live in a frame of the selected size. Zoom and pan change the view; the page keeps its layout width. The current screen stays visible while the next one loads.</p></li>
          <li><span class="n">04</span><h3>Markup bar</h3><p>Drawing tools and the handoff, under the frame. Select returns to using the page.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="toolbar-block" id="toolbar">
        <SectionHead kicker="Toolbar" title="Controls">
          The Actions toggle and the frame and screen controls are along the top; the markup tools and zoom are at the bottom of the canvas. Point at or tap a control to see what it does.
        </SectionHead>

        <ControlStrip groups={screenshots.toolbar}>
          <div class="controls-groups">
            <div class="controls-col">
              <div class="controls-group">
                <h3>Interaction</h3>
                <dl class="controls" data-group="actions">
                  <div data-label="Actions — let links navigate and forms submit"><dt>Actions</dt><dd>Off by default: links and form submissions are logged instead of followed. Turn it on to let links navigate and forms submit.</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <div class="controls-group">
                <h3>Only in some setups</h3>
                <dl class="controls">
                  <div><dt>State picker</dt><dd>Beside the screen’s name in the top bar when it has states, or under a Storybook lens, listing the title’s stories. Story picks reuse the loaded preview.</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>Lens switcher</dt><dd>Workbench for TypeScript previews, or Design for HTML pages, followed by additional implementations declared in <code>workbench.yaml</code>. See <a href="#lenses">Lenses</a>.</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>Preview controls</dt><dd>For Workbench previews: edit inputs, reset the selected state, inspect actions, and read documentation. Changes stay temporary.</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>Copy handoff</dt><dd>In VS Code only, at the end of the markup bar, beside a count of your marks. See <a href="#markup">Markup and handoff</a>.</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <div class="controls-group">
                <h3>Zoom</h3>
                <dl class="controls" data-group="zoom">
                  <div data-label="Recenter view"><dt>Recenter view</dt><dd>Brings the frame back to the middle of the canvas at the current zoom level.</dd></div>
                  <div data-label="Zoom out"><dt>Zoom out</dt><dd>Halves the zoom, to the next power of two. <kbd>⌘−</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl −</kbd>.</dd></div>
                  <div data-label="Zoom options"><dt>Zoom level</dt><dd>Opens zoom to fit, zoom to 100%, 50%, and 200%.</dd></div>
                  <div data-label="Zoom in"><dt>Zoom in</dt><dd>Doubles the zoom, to the next power of two. <kbd>⌘+</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl +</kbd>.</dd></div>
                </dl>
                <p class="controls-note">As in Figma: <kbd>⌘</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> with the scroll wheel, or a pinch, zooms at the pointer. Scroll to pan, or hold <kbd>Space</kbd> and drag. <kbd>⌘0</kbd> or <kbd>⇧0</kbd> zooms to 100%; <kbd>⇧1</kbd> zooms to fit. A new frame size fits itself to the window until you zoom or pan.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="controls-group">
              <h3>Markup</h3>
              <dl class="controls" data-group="markup">
                <div data-label="Select"><dt>Select</dt><dd>Use the page, and pick or move marks. <kbd>Esc</kbd> returns here from any drawing tool.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Scribble"><dt>Scribble</dt><dd>Freehand drawing.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Arrow"><dt>Arrow</dt><dd>An arrow.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Rectangle"><dt>Shapes</dt><dd>Rectangle by default. The small arrow beside it switches to a line or a circle.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Text"><dt>Text</dt><dd>A label placed on the screen.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Comment"><dt>Comment</dt><dd>A pinned note.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Undo last mark"><dt>Undo</dt><dd>Removes the last mark. <kbd>⌘Z</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl Z</kbd>.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Clear markup"><dt>Clear markup</dt><dd>Removes every mark on the screen.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Save screenshot"><dt>Save screenshot</dt><dd>Downloads a JPEG of the screen with your marks on it.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </div>
            <div class="controls-group">
              <h3>Frame and screen</h3>
              <dl class="controls" data-group="frame">
                <div data-label="Fit"><dt>Fit</dt><dd>The frame fills whatever space the workbench has.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Laptop, MacBook Pro 14, 1512 by 982 points"><dt>Laptop</dt><dd>1512 × 982, a 14-inch MacBook Pro. The canvas zooms out to show all of it; the page still lays out at its full width.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Mobile, iPhone 15 Pro, 393 by 852 points"><dt>Mobile</dt><dd>393 × 852, an iPhone 15 Pro.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Resizable viewport"><dt>Resizable</dt><dd>Drag an outside edge or corner to any size. The frame keeps that size until you change it.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Reload"><dt>Reload</dt><dd>Reloads the screen.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Open the source"><dt>Open the source</dt><dd>Lists the screen’s design file and any implementation code, and opens one in the editor.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Copy reference"><dt>Copy reference</dt><dd>Copies a short text reference to the screen, state, and lens, for pasting into a conversation. It doesn’t include a screenshot or your markup.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Open on its own"><dt>Open on its own</dt><dd>Opens the screen directly in a browser tab.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="More"><dt>More (⋯)</dt><dd><b>Configure pages</b>: a form for adding and removing sections and pages, editing labels and paths, and choosing each page’s viewports; it rewrites only the <code>sections</code> part of <code>workbench.yaml</code>. <b>Download design-system ZIP</b>: every screen’s files, the sources they depend on, and a reference screenshot at each viewport, in parts of 10 MB or less. When the window is narrow, the controls that don’t fit move here too.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </div>
          </div>
        </ControlStrip>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="features">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Feature id="states" kicker="States" title="Screens with several states">
          <p>A screen can have several states, such as a sign-in form when empty and after a wrong password. States are listed under their screen, like stories under a component in Storybook.</p>
          <p>An HTML page reads its state from <code>html[data-wb-state]</code>. Match CSS on <code>html[data-wb-state="error"]</code>, keep an element only in some states with <code>data-wb-state-only="error"</code>, or set a value with <code>data-wb-set-error="value=…"</code>. No scripts are added to your pages.</p>
          <CodeBlock file="workbench.yaml" code={statesYaml} copies="the states example" />
        <figure class="feature-media shot" slot="media">
          <img src={`${base}images/states.jpg`} width="1120" height="860" loading="lazy" alt="The Wrong password state of the sign-in page at mobile width: an error message above filled-in fields, with the password field outlined in red.">
        </figure>
      </Feature>

      <Feature id="markup" kicker="Markup and handoff" title="Annotate a screen and copy it for an agent">
          <p>Boxes, arrows, scribbles, and pinned comments are drawn over the running screen, which stays usable with Select. Select a mark to move it, nudge it with the arrow keys, or delete it with <kbd>Delete</kbd>.</p>
          <p>In VS Code, <b>Copy handoff</b> saves a screenshot to the project’s ignored <code>.canonic/.handoffs/</code> folder, clears the marks, and copies a text description of each mark to the clipboard: the element under it and the screen’s source file. Paste it into a coding agent’s chat.</p>
        <figure class="feature-media shot" slot="media">
          <img src={`${base}images/markup.jpg`} width="1120" height="860" loading="lazy" alt="The error state with markup: a red box around the error message, an arrow pointing to it, and a wavy line under the password field.">
        </figure>
      </Feature>

      <Feature id="lenses" kicker="Lenses" title="TypeScript previews and lenses">
          <p>Define components and screens in <code>.workbench.ts</code> files. Built-in adapters render HTML, React, Vue, Astro, and React Native Web, and you can register others. Each preview has named states, editable inputs, and an action log. A screen can also show its implementation as a lens: a Storybook story, a dev or staging URL, the iOS Simulator, or a macOS app window.</p>
          <p>Each state gives the preview its data: props, providers from a project-wide environment, or mocked responses to the page’s <code>fetch</code> and XMLHttpRequest calls. Links can open other previews; other navigation, such as a download or a route without a preview, is logged as an action. See <a href={`${pages.docs}preview-data/#choose-how-to-give-a-screen-its-data`}>Give a screen its data</a>.</p>
          <p>The selected lens stays as you change screens and is part of the address, so a copied link opens the same view. SVG icon sprites in TypeScript previews remain available as you switch states or return to a screen. For a Storybook or local server, a project can configure a start command and a readiness check; in a trusted workspace, Workbench runs the command in a terminal when the check fails. The design-system export includes a static viewer for the previews; Astro previews export their authored states only.</p>
          <img src={`${base}images/workbench-previews.jpg`} alt="Acme TypeScript preview with its named states and editable input controls" loading="lazy" />
        <div class="feature-media" slot="media">
          <CodeBlock file="address" code="#pages/sign-in.html:error@393~staging" copies="the address example" />
          <table class="address">
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>pages/sign-in.html</code></td><td>the screen’s design file</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>:error</code></td><td>the state, left out for the default</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>@393</code></td><td>the frame: a device width, <code>fit</code>, or a resizable size</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>~staging</code></td><td>the lens, left out for the design</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </Feature>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="setup" class="setup ink">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="setup-text">
        <span class="kicker">Setup</span>
        <h2>Configuration</h2>
        <p class="lede">Workbench activates in a folder with a <code>workbench.yaml</code> at its root. Your pages and preview definitions stay in your project; the extension adds nothing to them.</p>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock file="workbench.yaml" code={setupYaml} copies="the workbench.yaml example" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="install" class="install">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="install-head">
        <SectionHead kicker={`Install${version ? ` · v${version}` : ''}`} title="Install">
          In VS Code, search for <b>Canonic Workbench</b> in the Extensions view, or see it on the <a href={marketplace} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visual Studio Marketplace ↗</a>. For Cursor, Windsurf, and other VS Code forks, install the <code>.vsix</code> for your platform{downloads ? ':' : ' from GitHub.'}
        </SectionHead>
        <a class="text-link" href={pages.install}>Install guide →</a>
      </div>

      <DownloadCards downloads={downloads} />

      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <span class="n">01</span>
          <span class="title">Install a .vsix</span>
          <p>Only needed outside the Marketplace. In the Extensions view, click <b>⋯</b> and choose <b>Install from VSIX…</b>, or run:</p>
          <InstallCommand />
        </li>
        <li><span class="n">02</span><span class="title">Reload the window</span><p>If the editor was already open, run <b>Developer: Reload Window</b> from the Command Palette.</p></li>
        <li><span class="n">03</span><span class="title">Add workbench.yaml</span><p>Create it at the root of your project, like the <a href="#setup">example above</a>.</p></li>
        <li><span class="n">04</span><span class="title">Open the canvas</span><p>Open the <b>Workbench</b> view in the activity bar and pick a screen.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="footer"><span>Screens shown are an example project.</span></Fragment>
</Base>
